            <div xml:id="d589">

                <pb facs="0775" n="681" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg681"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.3361/24: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Soviet Deputy Commissar for Foreign Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Litvinov</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-01-31"><hi rendition="#italic">January 31,
                        1924</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 6:15 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>132. The Government of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics is in receipt of a
                    report from the authorities of Kamchatka to the effect that in September last
                    the United States warship <hi rendition="#italic">Bear</hi> entered Kolyuchin
                    [Bay?] in the Sinaeva Gulf without permission from the Soviet authorities. A
                    similar report has been received from the authorities of Batum stating that on
                    the 15th of January the American destroyer number 223 entered that port without
                    permission.</p>
                <pb facs="0776" n="682" xml:id="pg682"/>
                <p>In view of the fact that the circumstances under which the above-mentioned ships
                    entered Soviet harbors constitute a violation of the universally recognized
                    rules governing the entrance of warships into foreign ports, the Government of
                    the Soviet Socialist Republics finds itself obliged to protest against such
                    action on the part of ships under the direct control of the United States
                    Government and expects that the United States Government, professing the
                    necessity of strict observation of international laws, will take proper measures
                    to avert the repetition of such incidents in the future.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">M. Litvinoff</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
